--TEST--
a local declaration typed with a bare inline shape is refused naming the `type` alias form, while a block a variable happens to follow is left alone — `rule:types/shape-type`, `rule:types/object-literal`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:types/shape-type`: a shape is a type expression, so every declaration
// slot takes it bare but this one — a statement-initial `{` opens a block
// before it is anything else, so the type never reaches a declaration here.
// The refusal names the spelling that works, which is the one that rule's own
// example uses.
{x: int} $point;

// An optional key and a nested shape are the same declaration written in the
// same place: the tell is the variable after the *matched* `}` and not
// anything between the braces, so nesting changes nothing. The initializer is
// consumed with the declaration rather than re-read as a statement of its own.
{x?: int} $optional;
{outer: {inner: int}} $nested = {outer: {inner: 1}};

// The two spellings the scan must not claim, both of which compile clean: a
// block that a variable happens to follow, and `rule:types/type-alias`'s
// named form, which is the fix this error points at. The error count below is
// what asserts they were left alone — a scan that claimed either would report
// one more.
int $x = 0;
{ echo "b\n"; } $x = 1;

type Point = {x: int};
Point $p = {x: 1};
echo $p->x, "\n";

// A discarded object literal keeps the parenthesize refusal: no variable
// follows the `}`, so this tell answers no and `rule:types/object-literal`'s
// own error is still what an author reads.
{x: 1};
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0134]: a local declaration cannot be typed with a bare shape
